\chapter{Source files, tokens, and comments}
\label{ch:6}
\index{Source files, tokens, and comments}

Identifiers use ASCII letters, underscores, and digits after the first character. A hash begins a line comment except when the scanner recognizes the character-literal opener. Whitespace separates tokens. Source locations count byte columns, so a multi-byte UTF-8 value can make a token's byte column differ from its visual position. Preserve original bytes when reporting a diagnostic.

\section{A scanner boundary}
A scanner consumes a pointer and an explicit source length. The C++ entry point rejects embedded NUL and oversized source before the scanner runs. In the generated path, temporary token memory lives in an arena. The callback copies the token into C++ storage before the arena is destroyed. The fallback scanner supplies the same boundary and is tested against the generated scanner for token spelling and positions.

Some syntax distinctions depend on adjacency. For example, an adjacent qualified name such as \OnxCodeTerm{pkg.call} is distinct from spaced dotted syntax used in structural macros. When an import or a macro parses unexpectedly, print the tokens and check the actual characters and offsets. Changing precedence rules to repair a scanner issue risks creating inconsistent generated and fallback behavior.

